WEBVTT

00:03.600 --> 00:08.250
Hi! In this video, we walk you through
our NeurIPS 2026 paper, VI-CuRL:

00:08.270 --> 00:13.380
stabilizing verifier-independent
reinforcement learning for reasoning, through

00:13.400 --> 00:15.814
confidence-guided variance reduction.

00:17.133 --> 00:20.396
Let's start with how today's
reasoning models are trained.

00:20.416 --> 00:25.151
A large language model reads a math problem
and writes out a solution, step by step.

00:25.633 --> 00:28.334
A verifier then checks the final answer.

00:28.354 --> 00:32.414
If it is right, the model gets
a reward; if not, it doesn't.

00:32.434 --> 00:37.718
This is reinforcement learning with
verifiable rewards, or RLVR, and it powers

00:37.738 --> 00:40.030
reasoning models like DeepSeek-R1.

00:40.533 --> 00:45.179
But there is a catch. Reliable verifiers
are expensive to build, and for many

00:45.199 --> 00:47.724
open-ended tasks, they simply don't exist.

00:48.200 --> 00:53.380
Interestingly, recent studies suggest that
RLVR mostly draws out abilities the model

00:53.400 --> 00:56.537
already has, rather than
teaching it new knowledge.

00:56.557 --> 01:00.376
So a natural question is: can we
drop the verifier altogether?

01:01.633 --> 01:03.879
Verifier-free methods try exactly that.

01:03.899 --> 01:08.528
Instead of checking answers against the
ground truth, they reward the model with

01:08.548 --> 01:09.574
intrinsic signals.

01:10.067 --> 01:15.071
For example, majority vote treats the most
common answer among several samples as

01:15.091 --> 01:19.227
correct. Entropy-based rewards
encourage the model to be confident.

01:19.700 --> 01:21.920
Unfortunately, these signals are noisy.

01:21.940 --> 01:25.021
And noise in the reward
becomes noise in the gradient.

01:25.500 --> 01:29.257
The policy gets pushed in many
inconsistent directions at once.

01:29.277 --> 01:34.054
The gradient variance becomes destructively
large, and training often collapses.

01:34.533 --> 01:40.569
Here is how bad it gets. On Qwen2.5-Math
1.5B, majority-vote training with TTRL

01:40.589 --> 01:46.701
falls to an average accuracy of just 0.1
percent across six math benchmarks, and

01:46.721 --> 01:49.690
entropy-based RENT ends at 4.8 percent.

01:49.710 --> 01:54.136
That is far below the 20.3
percent the model started with.

01:55.400 --> 01:58.101
So how do we tame this
variance without a verifier?

01:58.121 --> 02:00.075
Our idea comes from how people learn.

02:00.567 --> 02:03.677
A good student doesn't start
with the hardest problems.

02:03.697 --> 02:08.344
They begin with the ones they feel sure
about, build a stable foundation, and then

02:08.364 --> 02:11.929
gradually take on everything
else. This is curriculum learning.

02:12.400 --> 02:18.258
Existing curriculum methods for RLVR, like
VCRL and AdaRFT, decide what is easy by

02:18.278 --> 02:23.633
using rewards from a verifier. We need
a signal that doesn't depend on one.

02:24.133 --> 02:26.465
Our answer is the model's own confidence.

02:26.967 --> 02:32.196
For each prompt, we look at the model's
sampled responses and measure the entropy of

02:32.216 --> 02:37.320
its next-token distribution at every step,
normalized by the log of the vocabulary

02:37.340 --> 02:42.569
size. Averaging gives an uncertainty, u of
x, and the confidence is simply one minus

02:42.589 --> 02:42.694
u.

02:43.167 --> 02:47.494
Low entropy means the model is sure
of itself, so confidence is high.

02:47.514 --> 02:50.644
Importantly, confidence is
never used as a reward.

02:50.664 --> 02:53.857
We only use it to decide
which prompts to train on.

02:55.133 --> 02:58.901
Here is how VI-CuRL works inside
a standard GRPO training loop.

02:59.400 --> 03:04.343
At each step, we sample a batch of prompts
and generate a group of responses for

03:04.363 --> 03:07.878
each one. Rewards come from
whatever source is available:

03:07.898 --> 03:10.235
a verifier, majority vote, or entropy.

03:10.733 --> 03:13.748
We then score every prompt by
its confidence, and rank them.

03:14.233 --> 03:18.035
Only the top fraction, beta t,
is kept for the gradient update.

03:18.055 --> 03:19.915
The rest are set aside for now.

03:20.400 --> 03:24.753
To keep the objective on the right scale,
the kept prompts are re-weighted by one

03:24.773 --> 03:25.401
over beta t.

03:25.900 --> 03:28.212
And here is the key: beta is annealed.

03:28.232 --> 03:32.999
We start by keeping only the most confident
20 percent, and gradually raise it

03:33.019 --> 03:36.190
until, by the end of training,
every prompt is used.

03:36.667 --> 03:40.250
So early on, we learn from a
stable, low-variance subset.

03:40.270 --> 03:42.716
Later, we cover the whole distribution.

03:44.000 --> 03:48.607
Now, you might worry: if we train on a
filtered subset, aren't we optimizing the

03:48.627 --> 03:52.655
wrong objective? That is the bias
side of the bias-variance trade-off.

03:53.133 --> 03:58.266
Our first theorem bounds this bias. If the
per-prompt loss is bounded by L max, then

03:58.286 --> 04:03.174
the gap between the curriculum objective
and the true objective is at most two L

04:03.194 --> 04:04.892
max, times one minus beta t.

04:05.367 --> 04:08.273
As beta t goes to one,
the gap shrinks to zero.

04:08.293 --> 04:12.009
So the curriculum objective
converges to the true objective.

04:12.500 --> 04:17.155
Notably, this guarantee does not need the
confidence score to be correct, or even

04:17.175 --> 04:20.502
calibrated. It only needs the
retention rate to reach one.

04:21.767 --> 04:26.733
What about variance? Our second theorem
splits the variance of the VI-CuRL gradient

04:26.753 --> 04:28.355
estimator into three parts.

04:28.833 --> 04:33.347
First, action variance: how much the
gradient changes when we sample different

04:33.367 --> 04:35.033
responses to the same prompt.

04:35.533 --> 04:40.206
Second, problem variance: how much the
average gradient differs from one prompt to

04:40.226 --> 04:40.664
another.

04:41.167 --> 04:46.071
And third, masking variance: the price
we pay for using only part of the batch.

04:46.091 --> 04:48.813
This term vanishes as beta t approaches one.

04:49.300 --> 04:51.826
The curriculum targets the first two terms.

04:51.846 --> 04:56.621
By keeping high-confidence prompts, the
selected gradients become more consistent

04:56.641 --> 05:00.824
and better aligned, so each update
points in a more reliable direction.

05:02.100 --> 05:04.186
Does this actually happen in practice?

05:04.206 --> 05:08.841
During training, we measured both variance
terms on the kept subset, and on the full

05:08.861 --> 05:09.174
batch.

05:09.667 --> 05:15.231
Early on, when beta is around 0.2, the
kept subset has 20 to 45 percent lower

05:15.251 --> 05:17.334
variance than the full batch.

05:17.833 --> 05:22.903
As beta grows toward one, the ratio climbs
back to one, just as the theory predicts.

05:22.923 --> 05:25.689
And action and problem
variance fall together.

05:26.967 --> 05:33.071
Now, the main results. We trained four
models, from 1.5 to 7 billion parameters, and

05:33.091 --> 05:38.393
evaluated them on six math benchmarks,
including AIME, AMC, MATH-500, and

05:38.413 --> 05:41.382
OlympiadBench, averaging over five seeds.

05:41.867 --> 05:46.681
In the verifier-free setting,
the difference is dramatic.

05:46.701 --> 05:53.382
On Qwen2.5-Math 1.5B with majority-vote
rewards, TTRL collapses to 0.1 percent,

05:53.402 --> 05:56.350
while VI-CuRL reaches 34.2 percent.

05:56.833 --> 06:04.214
With entropy rewards, VI-CuRL reaches 31.7
percent, compared with 4.8 for RENT and

06:04.234 --> 06:05.388
9.4 for EMPO.

06:05.867 --> 06:11.899
On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen 1.5B,
verifier-free VI-CuRL reaches 58.6 percent.

06:11.919 --> 06:18.266
That is higher even than standard training
with a ground-truth verifier, at 50.8.

06:18.767 --> 06:22.187
VI-CuRL also helps when
a verifier is available.

06:22.207 --> 06:28.064
On all four models, it achieves the best
average accuracy, ahead of the curriculum

06:28.084 --> 06:30.285
methods VCRL, AdaRFT, and SENT.

06:30.767 --> 06:36.183
And it goes beyond math. On general-knowledge
benchmarks such as MMLU-Pro and GPQA,

06:36.203 --> 06:41.095
verifier-free baselines break down,
while VI-CuRL stays stable and close to

06:41.115 --> 06:42.667
verifier-based training.

06:43.933 --> 06:47.149
But is it just filtering that
helps? We tested this directly.

06:47.633 --> 06:53.170
Keeping a random subset, the least
confident prompts, or the shortest responses

06:53.190 --> 06:58.937
gives little or no gain. Only confidence-guided
selection gives the big jump, from

06:58.957 --> 07:00.414
47.4 to 56.0 percent.

07:00.900 --> 07:05.966
Annealing matters too. A static filter
that always keeps 20 percent starts strong

07:05.986 --> 07:10.110
but collapses later, and soft
confidence weighting decays as well.

07:10.130 --> 07:14.693
Only the annealed schedule of VI-CuRL
stays stable through long training.

07:15.967 --> 07:21.033
To sum up. Verifier-free reinforcement
learning is attractive, but fragile, because

07:21.053 --> 07:24.098
noisy rewards cause
destructive gradient variance.

07:24.600 --> 07:28.324
VI-CuRL uses the model's own
confidence to build a curriculum:

07:28.344 --> 07:32.913
learn first from what the model is sure
about, then anneal to the full data.

07:33.400 --> 07:38.328
We prove that this is a principled
bias-variance trade-off that is asymptotically

07:38.348 --> 07:43.398
unbiased, and we show that it reduces
variance and prevents collapse across models,

07:43.418 --> 07:44.681
rewards, and domains.

07:45.167 --> 07:50.187
We hope VI-CuRL helps pave the way toward
scalable, self-improving reasoning models,

07:50.207 --> 07:52.827
wherever ground-truth supervision is scarce.

07:52.847 --> 07:56.007
Our code is available on
GitHub. Thanks for watching!
